  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kenya and Uganda Handwashing facilities 2021</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Research for Health in Humanitarian Crises (R2HC)
Study title: Effectiveness study of the Jengu Handwashing Unit to increase handwashing with soap among crisis-affected populations.
Focus of this research: This research aims to assess whether providing crisis-affected populations with increased access to desirable and durable handwashing facilities will lead to increases in handwashing behaviour. The research will also build evidence on the acceptability, usability, durability, cost-benefits, maintenance and sustainable local production of the facility.
While our research will focus on evaluating the Jengu handwashing facility, it will also generate insights about handwashing infrastructure and behaviour that are useful for other actors, given current gaps in the literature. This includes:
Whether investing in more robust and behaviour-centric infrastructure will result in improved behaviour and whether behavioural changes are sustained over time.
The relative effectiveness of different operational and maintenance modalities for handwashing infrastructure in crisis settings.
The rate of soap and water consumption from shared handwashing facilities and the longevity of handwashing infrastructure.&#xa0;
The requirements for, and impact of, a sustainable local supply chain for manufacturing and distributing handwashing facilities.</narrative>
  </description>
